explanation:
Regulation BI went into effect in mid-2020. It requires that customers receive a 2-page document at account opening called Form CRS – Customer Relationship Summary. Form CRS details:
- Whether the firm is acting as broker-dealer (charging commissions, markups/markdowns) or an investment adviser (charging a fixed fee) in the relationship;
- That the firm must adhere to a suitability standard when making recommendations as a broker-dealer; and that the firm must adhere to a tougher fiduciary standard when acting as an investment adviser;
- The fees and costs that the customer will pay;
- Any conflicts of interest that the firm may have in providing its services to the client; and
- Whether the firm or its associated persons has had any reportable disciplinary events.

If the firm presents a client with a package of documents at account opening, the Form CRS must be on the top. It must also be posted prominently on the firm's website.

Form CRS is only required to be provided to retail clients, including wealthy retail clients. It is not required to be provided to institutional clients, who are presumed to be sophisticated. The rule requires that the Form CRS be provided to retail clients at the earlier of making a recommendation or opening a new account.
